#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/sqlite.z", which is the one
place they are written. Every definition, field, arm and parameter there
carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" surfaces each on hover. This page is
the prose and the worked examples -- read it for how the unit is meant to be
used, and the source for the exact surface.

The #~"sqlite" unit is SQLite -- the library, version 3.54.0, vendored in
#~"vendor/sqlite" -- with an API modelled on Rust's #~"rusqlite": a
#~"Database" you open, #~"Statement"s you prepare on it, the #~"Row"s they
step through, and #~"Transaction"s. A program that reaches the unit has the
library compiled into it and linked statically (see
[#> to=building: Building]), so nothing need be installed to
build or run it.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"sqlite.openMemory",
#~"sqlite.Database"; there is no unqualified re-export.

#code language=zerolang: """
  main: function is {
      db: sqlite.openMemory
      if db.err then return
      r: (db.executeBatch
          sql: "create table person(id integer primary key, name text not null, age integer)"
      )
      if r.err then return
      ins: db.prepare sql: "insert into person(name, age) values (?1, ?2)"
      if ins.err then return
      ins.bindText parameter: 1 value: "Ada"
      ins.bindInteger parameter: 2 value: 36
      n: ins.execute
      if n.ok then print "inserted \{n} row, rowid \{db.lastInsertRowid}"
      ins.bindText parameter: 1 value: "Grace"
      ins.bindNull parameter: 2
      n2: ins.execute
      if n2.err then print "insert failed: \{n2.message}"
      q: db.prepare sql: "select name, age from person order by id"
      if q.err then return
      going: true
      for going loop {
          s: q.step
          match (
              s
          ) case row then {
              name: s.text column: 0
              age: s.integer column: 1
              match (
                  name
              ) case ok then {
                  match (
                      age
                  ) case ok then {
                      print "\{name} is \{age}"
                  } case err then {
                      print "\{name}, age unknown"
                  }
              } case err then print "no name"
          } case done then {
              going = false
          } case err then {
              print "step failed: \{s.message}"
              going = false
          }
      }
  }
"""

which prints

#code: """
  inserted 1 row, rowid 1
  Ada is 36
  Grace, age unknown
"""

#b:"Errors are values." Every call that can fail answers a #~"Result" or a
#~"Step" whose error is a [#> to=sqliteerror: #~"SqliteError"]: SQLite's
result code as an #~"errorcode" arm, its extended code, its message, and for
a syntax or name error the byte offset in the SQL. Nothing panics.


#--: Ownership

The rules that keep a C library's pointers valid are the language's own
locks, checked at compile time, as #~"rusqlite"'s are Rust's lifetimes. Each
handle that borrows from another keeps it #b:"pinned" while it lives:

#table cols="3 4 5":
  Handle           |Pins                          |So
  #~"Database"     |nothing; it is owned          |it closes when it goes out of scope
  #~"Statement"    |its connection, shared        |any number may live at once; #~"close" waits for them
  #~"Step" (a row) |its statement, exclusively    |the next #~"step" waits for the row to go
  #~"Transaction"  |its Database, exclusively     |statements run on the Transaction, not the Database
  #~"text" / #~"blob" views |their row               |a view never outlives the row it reads

Each of these is refused when it is written, not found at run time:

#code language=zerolang expect=E0200: """
  db: sqlite.openMemory
  if db.err then return
  q: db.prepare sql: "select 1"
  if q.err then return
  # refused: close locks db exclusively, and q still holds it shared
  c: db.close
  print "\{q.columnCount}"
"""

#code language=zerolang expect=E0200: """
  db: sqlite.openMemory
  if db.err then return
  with tx: db.transaction do {
      if tx.err then return
      # refused: tx holds db exclusively, so db cannot run a statement itself
      r: db.executeBatch sql: "select 1"
  }
"""

A #~"Statement" cannot be moved somewhere that outlives its #~"Database",
nor a #~"Row" somewhere that outlives its #~"Statement"; a getter on a
#~"Statement" (#~"q.integer") does not exist -- values are read from the row
a #~"step" answers.

#b:"Closing." A #~"Database" closes when it goes out of scope, as a #~"File"
does; every #~"Statement" prepared on it has gone by then, since each pins
it. An explicit #~"close" is how a program hears
what closing reported; a second #~"close" answers ok, and a #~"Database" used
after it answers #~"misuse". A #~"Statement" is finalized when it goes out of
scope.


#--: Transactions

#~"db.transaction" begins one and answers the #~"Transaction" that pins the
#~"Database" until it ends. It ends three ways: #~"commit", #~"rollback", or
going out of scope -- which #b:"rolls back". So the natural form is a
#~"with" block, and any #~"return" or error that leaves it before
#~"commit" undoes the work:

#code language=zerolang: """
  # transfer -- move `amount` between two accounts, all or nothing
  transfer: function {db: sqlite.Database from: i64 to: i64 amount: i64} out bool is {
      with tx: (db.transaction mode: sqlite.transactionmode.immediate) do {
          if tx.err then return false
          q: tx.prepare sql: "update account set balance = balance + ?2 where id = ?1"
          if q.err then return false
          q.bindInteger parameter: 1 value: from
          q.bindInteger parameter: 2 value: 0 - amount
          a: q.execute
          if a.err then return false          # rolled back
          q.bindInteger parameter: 1 value: to
          q.bindInteger parameter: 2 value: amount
          b: q.execute
          if b.err then return false          # rolled back
          q.drop
          c: tx.commit
          return c.ok
      }
  }
"""

The parentheses around a #~"transaction" call with arguments are required:
#~"with" reads its value up to #~"do", and a bare
#~"db.transaction mode: ..." would leave #~"mode:" to the #~"with". A call
with no arguments needs none: #~"with tx: db.transaction do".

A #~"Statement" prepared on the #~"Transaction" pins it, so #~"commit" waits
for it -- #~"q.drop" above ends it first. A #~"COMMIT" that fails (#~"busy",
a deferred foreign-key violation) leaves the transaction open, to retry or
roll back; once ended, the #~"Transaction"'s #~"executeBatch", #~"prepare",
#~"commit" and #~"rollback" answer #~"misuse". A second #~"BEGIN" inside one
is SQLite's error, reported as such. The [#> to=transactionmode: mode] says
when the write lock is taken.

#b:"Code that runs either way." #~"Database" and #~"Transaction" both conform
to the [#> to=connection: #~"Connection"] protocol, so a function written
against #~"Connection.view" runs on either:

#code language=zerolang: """
  count: function {c: sqlite.Connection.view} out i64 is {
      q: c.prepare sql: "select count(*) from account"
      if q.err then return -1
      s: q.step
      match s case row then return ((s.integer column: 0).or d: -1) else return -1
  }
"""

#--: Threads

A connection is used by one thread at a time -- reads included, since SQLite
keeps state on the connection for every statement -- so #~"Database",
#~"Transaction", #~"Statement" and #~"Row" conform to
#~"thread.Unshareable". A connection may be moved to a thread
(#~"db.take"), which owns it from then on, or borrowed by one
(#~"db.borrow"), which keeps its owner off it until the join. A task holding a
view of one is refused, as is one holding a Statement (which views its
connection): the owner would go on using it beside the thread. To give
several threads one database, give each its own connection -- #~"open" the
same file on each -- or share one connection through a #~"sync.Mutex",
taking the lock for each turn. An #~"RwLock" will not do: its readers run at
once.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # Adder -- inserts through its handle to the shared connection
  Adder: class {
      # a handle to the lock that owns the connection
      m: sync.Mutex sqlite.Database
  } as {
      task: thread.Task null
      call: function {:this} is {
          g: this.m.lock
          r: g.value.executeBatch sql: "insert into t values (1)"
          if r.err then print "insert failed"
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      db: sqlite.openMemory
      if db.err then return
      c: db.executeBatch sql: "create table t(x)"
      m: (sync.Mutex sqlite.Database).create value: db.take
      {
          a: thread.spawn task: (Adder m: m.share)
          b: thread.spawn task: (Adder m: m.share)
      }
      print "both inserted"
  }
"""

A program that starts a thread builds SQLite multi-thread
(#~"SQLITE_THREADSAFE=2"): SQLite's own shared state is locked, and each
connection is left to the one thread the types allow.


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Types"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=database: #b:"Database"] -- #~"class as { :Connection  executeBatch  prepare  changes  totalChanges  lastInsertRowid  isAutocommit  busyTimeout  transaction  close }"]
  [#*: [#> to=transaction: #b:"Transaction"] -- #~"class as { :Connection  executeBatch  prepare  changes  totalChanges  lastInsertRowid  isAutocommit  commit  rollback }"]
  [#*: [#> to=connection: #b:"Connection"] -- #~"protocol { executeBatch  prepare  changes  totalChanges  lastInsertRowid  isAutocommit }"]
  [#*: [#> to=statement: #b:"Statement"] -- #~"class as { bind...  parameterIndex  parameterCount  column...  step  execute  reset  clearBindings  sql }"]
  [#*: [#> to=step: #b:"Step"] -- #~"union { row: Row  done: null  err: SqliteError }"]
  [#*: [#> to=row: #b:"Row"] -- #~"class as { integer  real  text  blob  value  columnType  columnCount  columnName }"]
  [#*: [#> to=value: #b:"Value"] -- #~"union { null  integer: i64  real: f64  text: String  blob: Bytes }"]
  [#*: [#> to=sqliteerror: #b:"SqliteError"] -- #~"class { code: errorcode  extendedCode: i32  message: String  offset: optionval u64 }"]
  [#*: [#> to=errorcode: #b:"errorcode"] -- SQLite's primary result codes]
  [#*: [#> to=columntype: #b:"columntype"] / [#> to=columnerror: #b:"columnerror"] -- a column's storage class; why a getter could not answer]
  [#*: [#> to=openmode: #b:"openmode"] / [#> to=transactionmode: #b:"transactionmode"] -- how to open a file; how a transaction locks]

#p: #b:"Functions"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=open: #b:"sqlite.open"] -- #~"function {path: StringView mode: openmode.create} out (Result Database E: SqliteError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=openmemory: #b:"sqlite.openMemory"] -- #~"function out (Result Database E: SqliteError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=version: #b:"sqlite.version"] -- #~"function out String"]


#--: database


An open connection, owned: it closes when it goes out of scope. #~"open"
gives it SQLite's extended result codes and a 5 second busy timeout.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"executeBatch sql:" -- runs one or more statements separated by
    #~";", stopping at the first failure; result rows are discarded]
  [#*: #~"prepare sql:" -- compiles exactly one statement into a
    #~"Statement" pinning the #~"Database". SQL with a second statement after
    the first is refused (a trailing comment is not one); SQL holding no
    statement at all -- empty, a comment, a lone #~";" -- prepares and steps
    straight to #~"done"]
  [#*: #~"changes" / #~"totalChanges" -- rows the last INSERT, UPDATE or
    DELETE changed; rows changed since the connection opened, triggers'
    included]
  [#*: #~"lastInsertRowid" -- the rowid of the last row inserted, 0 before any]
  [#*: #~"isAutocommit" -- true when no transaction is open]
  [#*: #~"busyTimeout milliseconds:" -- how long a statement meeting
    another connection's lock waits before failing with #~"busy"; 0 fails at
    once]
  [#*: #~"transaction mode:" -- begins a [#> to=transaction: #~"Transaction"]]
  [#*: #~"close" -- closes now and answers what closing reported]


#--: transaction


An open transaction, pinning its #~"Database" exclusively. It runs
statements as the #~"Database" does (it is a #~"Connection"), and ends with
#~"commit", #~"rollback", or going out of scope, which rolls back. See
[#> to=transactions: Transactions].


#--: connection


The protocol #~"Database" and #~"Transaction" conform to:
#~"executeBatch", #~"prepare", #~"changes", #~"totalChanges",
#~"lastInsertRowid" and #~"isAutocommit". Write a helper against
#~"Connection.view" and it runs inside a transaction or outside one.


#--: statement


A compiled statement, owned, pinning the connection it was prepared on.
Parameters are 1-based -- #~"?1", or a named #~":id" / #~"@id" / #~"$id"
whose index #~"parameterIndex" finds -- and columns 0-based.

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Binding." #~"bindInteger", #~"bindReal", #~"bindText",
    #~"bindBlob", #~"bindNull" and #~"bindValue" (a #~"Value") set one
    parameter; text and blobs are copied. A binding stays until rebound or
    #~"clearBindings"; an unbound parameter is NULL. Binding a statement
    that has run resets it first. A bind that fails -- no such parameter --
    is reported by the next #~"step" or #~"execute", naming the parameter]
  [#*: #b:"Lists." #~"bindIntegerList", #~"bindRealList",
    #~"bindTextList" and #~"bindBlobList" bind a whole list as the rows of
    SQLite's #~"carray" table: #~"where id in carray(?1)" tests membership
    with one statement, however long the list. An empty list matches
    nothing. #~"bindTextList" passes each string as a C string, so one
    holding a NUL byte ends there]
  [#*: #b:"Running." #~"step" answers the next [#> to=step: #~"Step"];
    #~"execute" runs to completion and answers how many rows changed,
    refusing a statement that yields a row; #~"reset" rewinds it, keeping
    its bindings]
  [#*: #b:"Describing." #~"parameterCount", #~"columnCount",
    #~"columnName" (the #~"AS" alias, else SQLite's name), and the column
    metadata #~"columnDeclaredType", #~"columnTableName",
    #~"columnOriginName", #~"columnDatabaseName" -- each #~"null" for an
    expression column; #~"sql" is the statement's text]

#code language=zerolang: """
  db: sqlite.openMemory
  if db.err then return
  ids: ListVal i64
  ids.append 1
  ids.append 2
  sel: db.prepare sql: "select count(*) from person where id in carray(?1)"
  if sel.err then return
  sel.bindIntegerList parameter: 1 values: ids
"""


#--: step


What #~"step" answers: #~"row" with the next [#> to=row: #~"Row"],
#~"done" when the statement has run to completion, or #~"err" with the
[#> to=sqliteerror: #~"SqliteError"]. The row pins the statement
exclusively: the next #~"step" is taken once this #~"Step" has gone.


#--: row


One result row, read by column. Its getters are #b:"strict", as
#~"rusqlite"'s are, and convert only what cannot lose:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"integer" -- INTEGER only; a REAL is #~"mismatch", not truncated]
  [#*: #~"real" -- REAL, or an INTEGER converted]
  [#*: #~"text" -- TEXT, checked to be UTF-8 (#~"encoding" when it is
    not), as a #~"StringView" into the row]
  [#*: #~"blob" -- BLOB, as a #~"ByteView" into the row]
  [#*: #~"value" -- whatever the column holds, copied out as a
    [#> to=value: #~"Value"]]
  [#*: #~"columnType", #~"columnCount", #~"columnName"]

NULL answers #~"columnerror.null", another storage class #~"mismatch", a
column the row lacks #~"range". The #~"text" and #~"blob" views live as long
as the row; copy one (#~".copy") to keep it.


#--: value


A column's value, owned: #~"null", #~"integer" (#~"i64"), #~"real"
(#~"f64"), #~"text" (#~"String") or #~"blob" (#~"Bytes") -- SQLite's five
storage classes. #~"Row.value" copies one out; #~"Statement.bindValue" binds
one.


#--: sqliteerror


Why an SQLite call failed: #~"code", the primary result code as an
[#> to=errorcode: #~"errorcode"] arm; #~"extendedCode", SQLite's extended
code, which refines it (a UNIQUE violation within #~"constraint");
#~"message", SQLite's own description; and #~"offset", the byte offset into
the SQL of a syntax or name error -- #~"select nosuchfunction(1)" fails at 7.


#--: errorcode


SQLite's primary result codes, each arm the code of the same name
(#~"busy" is #~"SQLITE_BUSY"): #~"error", #~"internal", #~"permission",
#~"abort", #~"busy", #~"locked", #~"noMemory", #~"readOnly",
#~"interrupt", #~"ioError", #~"corrupt", #~"notFound", #~"full",
#~"cannotOpen", #~"lockProtocol" (SQLITE_PROTOCOL; #~"protocol" is a
keyword), #~"schema", #~"tooBig", #~"constraint", #~"mismatch",
#~"misuse", #~"noLargeFileSupport", #~"authorization", #~"format",
#~"range", #~"notADatabase", #~"notice", #~"warning".


#--: columntype


A column's storage class: #~"null", #~"integer", #~"real", #~"text",
#~"blob".


#--: columnerror


Why a #~"Row" getter could not answer: #~"null" (the column is NULL),
#~"mismatch" (another storage class), #~"range" (no such column),
#~"encoding" (TEXT that is not UTF-8).


#--: openmode


How [#> to=open: #~"open"] opens a file: #~"create" (read and write,
creating it; the default), #~"readWrite" (an existing file), #~"readOnly".


#--: transactionmode


When a transaction takes its locks -- SQLite's #~"BEGIN DEFERRED",
#~"IMMEDIATE", #~"EXCLUSIVE". #~"deferred" (the default) locks as it first
reads and then writes, so a write can still meet #~"busy";
#~"immediate" takes the write lock at #~"BEGIN"; #~"exclusive" also keeps
readers out outside WAL mode.


#--: open


Open the database file at #~"path", creating it in #~"create" mode. A file
that cannot be opened as #~"mode" asks fails with #~"cannotOpen", the
message naming it.


#--: openmemory


A new, empty database in memory, gone when it closes.


#--: version


The SQLite library's version, #~"3.54.0".


#--: Building

#---: The object cache

SQLite is one C file of about 9.5 MB. A program that reaches the unit
records it on a #~"zcsrc:" header line of its emitted C, and #~"zc build" /
#~"zc run" compile it once into the #b:"object cache" -- keyed by the
source, the compiler that links, the target and the flags -- and link that
object from then on. The first build pays the compile: about 15 s under
gcc -O2, well under a second under tcc. A cross build (#~"--target
aarch64-linux-gnu") compiles its own object with the cross compiler. A user
unit that happens to be named #~"sqlite" builds nothing: the rows that
bring SQLite in apply only to the unit that declares its natives.

The cache is #~"$ZC_OBJ_CACHE", else #~"zerolang/obj" under the user's
cache directory; deleting it is always safe. #~"zc cobj <file.c>" builds
the objects a hand-run compiler needs and prints their paths. See
[#> to=./zc.html: zc], C sources a unit builds.

#---: Compile options

The library is compiled #~"-O2 -DNDEBUG -w" with the same options on every
target, whatever the program is built with:

#ul:
  [#*: #~"SQLITE_THREADSAFE=0" -- one thread; #~"SQLITE_THREADSAFE=2" in a
    program that starts a thread (see [#> to=threads: Threads])]
  [#*: #~"SQLITE_DQS=0" -- a double-quoted string is an identifier, never
    a string literal]
  [#*: #~"SQLITE_OMIT_LOAD_EXTENSION", #~"SQLITE_OMIT_DEPRECATED",
    #~"SQLITE_OMIT_SHARED_CACHE", #~"SQLITE_OMIT_PROGRESS_CALLBACK"]
  [#*: #~"SQLITE_DEFAULT_MEMSTATUS=0", #~"SQLITE_DEFAULT_WAL_SYNCHRONOUS=1",
    #~"SQLITE_LIKE_DOESNT_MATCH_BLOBS", #~"SQLITE_MAX_EXPR_DEPTH=0",
    #~"SQLITE_STRICT_SUBTYPE=1"]
  [#*: enabled: FTS5, R*Tree, the math functions, percentile, the
    #~"dbstat" table, column metadata, #~"carray"; JSON and deserialize are
    on by default]

Every other behaviour is SQLite's default -- foreign keys, for one, are off
until a program runs #~"pragma foreign_keys = on". Each option's reason is
in #~"vendor/sqlite/VERSION.md".

#---: Upgrading

#~"vendor/sqlite/VERSION.md" holds the procedure: read the release's
change log, run #~"tools/vendor-sqlite/vendor-sqlite.sh <version> <year>
<sha3-256>" (which refuses a download whose hash does not match), update
the version record, and run #~"make ci" -- its #~"sqlite-decls-guard"
compiles the unit's C declarations against the new #~"sqlite3.h", and every
sqlite program runs under gcc, clang, tcc and an aarch64 build. One commit.

#---: Not yet

SQL functions, collations and hooks written in zerolang need calls from C
into zerolang, which the language does not have yet. Savepoints, cached
statements, column access by name, and the backup, blob and serialize APIs
are not bound yet.
